2 DATE 16 NOV 1801
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Piotr Mikołaj Trubecki h. Pogoń Litewska''' or '''Peter Nicolaewitch Prince Troubetzkoy''' was born on November 29/18, 1773, in the illustrious estate of Neskuchnoye (Nonboring), located in what is today the Yak
2 CONC imanka district of Moscow, within the expansive Russian Empire. He was born into the esteemed Troubetzkoy family, known for their noble lineage and influential contributions to Lithuanian, Polish, and Russian society. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter was the son of Nicolay Nikititch Prince Troubetzkoy and Warwara Alexandrowna Tcherkasskaya, born into a family deeply rooted in their noble heritage. Nicolay Nikititch and Warwara Alexandrowna, his parents, instilled in him 
2 CONC a strong sense of his family's illustrious history and the values associated with nobility. However, fate had a different plan for Peter's upbringing. The course of Peter's upbringing took an unexpected turn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1780, when Peter was just a young boy, his parents made the difficult decision to send him to Gdańsk, a city located by the sea in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. They believed that this move would provide him with a unique
2 CONC  opportunity to grow up and receive an education in a different cultural environment. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Under their care, Peter, or Piotr as he was known during his formative years, embarked on a new chapter of his life, far from the familiar surroundings of his homeland. In Gdańsk, he became like foster son to his distant relative,
2 CONC  Franciczek Trubecki, and his wife. This arrangement provided Piotr with a loving and nurturing family environment, ensuring that he received the care and guidance necessary for his upbringing. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the Trubecki family, Piotr found a kindred spirit in Franciczek's son, Fryderyk. Despite the two-year age difference, they quickly formed a close bond, becoming inseparable playmates and the best of friends. Fryderyk, born d
2 CONC uring his parents' visit to Michał Kleofas Ogiński at the grand Bieszenkowicze Palace in the Hrabstwo Bieszenkowicki region of Województwo Połockie, was a constant companion for young Piotr during their time together in Gdańsk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout his stay in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, Piotr experienced a blend of cultures and perspectives, fostering a broader understanding of the World. The guidance and support provided by his foster family, alongside t
2 CONC he companionship of his dear friend Fryderyk, played a vital role in shaping Piotr's character and nurturing his intellectual growth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Piotr grew up in a privileged environment and received a comprehensive education that fostered his intellectual and scholarly pursuits. As Piotr matured, he displayed a keen intellect and a natural aptitude for academic pursuits. 
2 CONC He thirsted for knowledge and dedicated himself to his studies, honing his intellect and broadening his understanding of various fields of study. His insatiable curiosity led him to excel in his education, setting the stage for a 
2 CONC promising future. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The years spent in Gdańsk were formative for Piotr, leaving an indelible mark on his identity and worldview. The bonds forged during his upbringing would have a lasting impact, shaping his values, and influencing his future endeav
2 CONC ors. As he prepared to transition into adulthood, Piotr carried with him the cherished memories and lessons learned from his time in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. These experiences would lay the foundation for the remarkable
2 CONC  journey that awaited him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 23, 1793, the Kingdom of Prussia annexed Gdańsk in the Second Partition of Poland. Amidst the political turmoil and discontent that followed the annexation of Gdańsk by the Kingdom of Prussia, the fate of young Piotr to
2 CONC ok a significant turn. The opposition from both the Polish and German-speaking population to the Prussian takeover echoed throughout the city, with many longing for Gdańsk to remain a part of Poland. Among those who expressed thei
2 CONC r dissent was the mayor of the city, who stepped down from his office in protest against the annexation. Another prominent figure, Jan Uphagen, a notable city councilor, renowned historian, and art collector, resigned as a poignan
2 CONC t gesture of opposition to the Prussian rule. Uphagen's Baroque house, which now stands as a museum, serves as a testament to his dedication to preserving Polish heritage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the midst of these uncertain times, the family of Franciczek Trubecki, recognizing the need for a safe haven, sought refuge in the care of Adam Kazimierz Czartoryski and his wife, Izabela Dorota Fortunata Czartoryska, née Gräfi
2 CONC n von Flemming, who graciously opened their arms to those affected by the political upheaval. The Trubecki family found solace and support in the embrace of the Czartoryski family, who provided them with a new home and a sense of 
2 CONC stability. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the move to the Sieniawa Palace in the Przemyśl County of the Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria, part of the Austrian Empire, Piotr found himself in a different environment, far from the city he once called home. Adjusting to 
2 CONC his new surroundings, Piotr navigated the challenges of acclimating to a new culture and language. Yet, in the midst of change, he remained grounded by the love and care bestowed upon him by his foster family and the enduring bond
2 CONC  he shared with his best friend, Fryderyk, who accompanied him on this new chapter of their lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Trubecki family's relocation to Sieniawa Palace not only offered them a place of refuge but also allowed Piotr to continue his intellectual growth and exploration. Surrounded by the rich history and art that adorned the palace
2 CONC  walls, Piotr's thirst for knowledge found new avenues of fulfillment. Inspired by the resilience and strength exhibited by those around him, he embraced his noble heritage and the values associated with it, instilled in him by hi
2 CONC s parents from a young age. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As Piotr settled into his new life, he carried with him the memories of Gdańsk, a city he cherished and a place that remained close to his heart. The echoes of protest against the Prussian annexation reverberated within him, fueli
2 CONC ng his desire to make a difference and contribute to the preservation of Polish identity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Little did Piotr know that his journey was far from over. The experiences and influences he encountered in the Sieniawa Palace would shape his character and lay the foundation for the remarkable path that awaited him. With each pa
2 CONC ssing day, Piotr grew not only in stature but also in wisdom, preparing himself for the challenges and triumphs that would define his legacy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the midst of these uncertain times, the Trubecki family, made the decision to send young Piotr to live with his parents in Moscow. After completing his education, Piotr, now known as Peter Nicolaewitch Prince Troubetzkoy, embar
2 CONC ked on a career in academia and joined Moscow University as an Assistant Director. His role at the university allowed him to engage with prominent scholars and contribute to the advancement of knowledge in various fields. As a Col
2 CONC legiate Advisor, he provided guidance and support to students and faculty members, ensuring the smooth functioning of the institution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In due course, Peter's path crossed with Nadezhda Ivanovna Pestova, a woman of grace, intelligence, and virtue. Their connection was instantaneous, and they soon found solace and love in each other's company. Recognizing their sha
2 CONC red values and ambitions, Peter and Nadezhda joined in matrimony, embarking on a lifelong journey together. Peter and Nadezhda's union blessed them with four children, each unique in their own right. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the tumultuous period of political intrigue and conspiracies in the Russian Empire, Peter found himself indirectly entangled in the events that would shape the future of the nation. As the son of Nicolay Troubetzkoy, a Mart
2 CONC inist and a friend of Nikolay Ivanovitch Novikov, Peter grew amidst the teachings of Christian mysticism and esoteric Christianity. These philosophies instilled in him a profound sense of spiritual introspection and a belief in th
2 CONC e potential for human reintegration with the divine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, the discontent among the Russian nobility and aristocracy towards Emperor Paul I continued to simmer. Already in the years 1797–1799, a conspiracy against the emperor was developing. It was attended by the heir to the t
2 CONC hrone, Alexander, his wife Elżbieta Aleksiejewna, Adam Jerzy Czartoryski, Nikolai Nowosilcow, Peter Stroganov, Wiktor Kochubey and others. It was not an organized action and was not a real threat to the ruler, but it became an imp
2 CONC ortant warning signal for him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter remained somewhat detached from the direct conspiracies against the ruler but was well aware of the sentiments pervading the upper echelons of society. Paul's attempts to diminish the privileges of the nobility and his contr
2 CONC oversial policies had alienated many of his trusted advisors, leading to a growing sense of dissatisfaction among the elites. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1801, another conspiracy took shape, spearheaded by notable figures such as Peter Ludwig von der Pahlen, Platon Zubov, Nikolai Zubov, and General Levin August von Bennigsen, among others. These influential individuals, includin
2 CONC g Peter's acquaintances, demanded that Paul I abdicate the throne in favor of his son, Alexander. When the emperor refused, the conspirators resorted to drastic measures. On the fateful night of March 23/11, a band of dismissed of
2 CONC ficers stormed into St. Michael's Castle, where Paul resided. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter was not present during the assassination of Paul I. Instead, he received word of the shocking events that unfolded that night. General Nikolay Zubov, one of the assassins, announced Alexander's accession to the throne and im
2 CONC plored him to take up the mantle of leadership. Alexander, though complicit in the plot, had not anticipated that his father's overthrow would result in such a violent act. The guilt and remorse that accompanied his ascension to p
2 CONC ower weighed heavily on the young emperor's conscience. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As Alexander I assumed the throne, he undertook a series of reforms aimed at rectifying the perceived wrongs committed by his father's regime. Determined to restore stability and justice, Alexander issued decrees that rehabilitate
2 CONC d individuals removed from offices and the army by Paul I. He sought to increase the influence of the Senate, abolish peasant serfdom in the Baltic provinces, and liberalize foreign trade. His first decrees concerned the abolition
2 CONC  of book censorship, the prohibition of torture in investigations, and reforms in education. Universities in Charkiw and Kazan were established, as well as the reactivated Wilno University with Polish as the language of instructio
2 CONC n. Furthermore, Alexander showed a particular affinity towards the Polish people, with his close friend Adam Jerzy Czartoryski serving as Minister of Foreign Affairs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As Peter witnessed the transformation and reform efforts undertaken by his childhood friend, Alexander I, he couldn't help but feel a mix of admiration and trepidation. The weight of the conspiracies, the turmoil of the empire, an
2 CONC d the profound changes sweeping through Russia left an indelible mark on Peter's consciousness. It was a time of introspection and reflection, as he contemplated his own role and purpose within the evolving landscape of the Russia
2 CONC n Empire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beyond his administrative responsibilities, Peter Troubetzkoy had a passion for literature and poetry. In 1801, he composed an ode titled „Ode on the Most Joyful Day of the Accession of His Imperial Majesty, the Autocrat of All Ru
2 CONC ssia, Alexander the First, to the Ancestral Throne.“ This work, published shortly before his untimely demise, celebrated the ascension of Emperor Alexander I to the Russian throne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tragedy struck the life of Peter Nicolaewitch Prince Troubetzkoy on that fateful day of November 28/16, 1801. The circumstances surrounding his untimely demise remain shrouded in mystery, leaving behind questions that may never be
2 CONC  answered. Peter, a man of noble heritage, academic pursuits, and familial devotion, was abruptly taken from the world, leaving a void in the hearts of those who knew and loved him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The details of Peter's death are elusive, lost in the annals of history. Whether it was an act of political intrigue, an unfortunate accident, or a sinister plot, the truth may forever remain obscured. What remains certain is that
2 CONC  his passing on that somber November day sent shockwaves through the hearts of his family, friends, and the community that held him in high regard.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the wake of this tragic event, Peter Nicolaewitch Prince Troubetzkoy was laid to rest in the peaceful and serene 4th section of the cemetery at the Donskoy Monastery in Moscow. Surrounded by the hallowed grounds that hosted the
2 CONC  remains of many notable figures throughout Russian history, Peter found his final resting place, a poignant reminder of his significance in the tapestry of the nation's past.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though his life was cut short, Peter's memory endured in the hearts and minds of those who cherished him. His noble name and spirit of excellence lived on through his descendants, carrying forth his legacy with pride and reverence
2 CONC . The void left by his absence would forever be felt, a testament to the impact he had made in his all-too-brief existence.
2 CONT
2 CONT As time passed, the details of Peter's death may have faded, but the essence of his character and the imprint he left on the lives he touched remained indelible. His contributions to the academic world, his devotion to his family,
2 CONC  and his embrace of his noble heritage served as a lasting testament to his remarkable journey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, as we reflect upon the life of Peter Nicolaewitch Prince Troubetzkoy, we remember the brilliance of his mind, the warmth of his heart, and the untimely end that stole him away from a world that still needed his presence. Ma
2 CONC y his soul find eternal peace in the realms beyond, and may his legacy continue to inspire generations to come. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Trubetskoy was survived by his wife, Nadezhda Ivanovna Pestova, with whom he had a loving and supportive relationship. Together, they raised four children who would go on to leave their mark on society. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their eldest daughter, Anna, inherited her parents' passion for the arts. Gifted with creativity and a refined taste, she embraced the cultural scene of her time, becoming a respected patroness of the arts and contributing to the 
2 CONC flourishing artistic landscape of the era. Anna Petrovna Kozhukhova, the eldest daughter, led a life marked by intellectual curiosity and artistic inclinations. She embraced the cultural scene of her time and became known for her 
2 CONC patronage of the arts, supporting talented individuals and contributing to the flourishing cultural landscape of the era. Unfortunately, her life was cut short, and she passed away in 1827, leaving behind a legacy of artistic appr
2 CONC eciation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yuri, the firstborn son, inherited his father's noble heritage and demonstrated unwavering dedication to his family and country. He grew to be a remarkable individual, embodying the values of honor, bravery, and patriotism. Yuri a
2 CONC ctively participated in the military campaigns of the Russian Empire, leaving a mark on the war of his time. In recognition of his valor and leadership, he rose to the rank of Captain in the Life Guards Grenadier Regiment. Yuri Pe
2 CONC trovitch Troubetzkoy, the first son, distinguished himself as a participant in the war of 1812, displaying valor and bravery in the face of adversity. Following the war, he served as a Captain in the prestigious Life Guards Grenad
2 CONC ier Regiment from 1823 to 1825. Yuri married Ludwika Czajkowska of the Jastrzębiec coat of arms, forming a union that blended noble families. Collegiate assessor and gentleman serving at the Imperial Moscow Post Office, baptismal 
2 CONC recipient (17.8.1844, Moscow). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grigori, the second son, inherited his father's intellectual acumen and pursued a career in government service. Imbued with a deep sense of duty and a commitment to public administration, Grigori's exemplary service earned him the
2 CONC  distinguished title of Acting State Councilor. Grigori Petrowitch Troubetzkoy, second son, followed in his father's footsteps. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The youngest daughter, Helena, led a more private life. Although information about her endeavors remains scarce, it can be inferred that she cherished her family and pursued her own personal passions. While information about Helen
2 CONC a Trubecka h. Pogoń Litewska is scarce, it can be inferred that she likely led a private life, away from the public eye. Her contributions and pursuits remain relatively unknown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Nicolaewitch Prince Troubetzkoy or Piotr Mikołaj Trubecki h. Pogoń Litewska, though his life was brief, left a lasting impact on his family and the academic world of his time. His dedication to education, his literary endeav
2 CONC ors, and his role as an Assistant Director at Moscow University showcase his commitment to the pursuit of knowledge and the betterment of society. His final resting place at the Donskoy Monastery stands as a testament to his endur
2 CONC ing memory within Russian, Polish, and Lithuanian history. 
2 CONT
