1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Nester Trubecki''' (1832 Free City of Cracow – after 1919) was a landowner and revolutionary, anarchist, Prince. Nester Trubecki was born in The Free City of Cracow as the son of Prince Grigori Troubetzkoy (a fr
2 CONC eemason) and a Polish szlachta lady M. Kalinowska h. Kalinowa. They met in Saint Petersburg; after that they moved to Kraków and Nester was born there. The Congress of Vienna (1815) restored the partition of Poland, but gave Krakó
2 CONC w its independence. The city became again the focus of the struggle for national sovereignty in 1846, during the Kraków Uprising. The uprising failed to spread outside the city to other Polish-inhabited lands, and was put down, re
2 CONC sulting in Kraków's annexation by Austrian Empire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After graduating from The Jagiellonian University in 1857 Trubecki went to Vienna, where he started studying at the University of Vienna and got involved in several Polish students' conspiracies and secret cultural societies. Afte
2 CONC r exmatriculation in 1859 Trubecki returned to the area of Kraków, The Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria, where he continued to act as a revolutionary. In his literary work, Trubecki underlined the need to liberate all peoples of t
2 CONC he former Republic of the Two Nations from Imperial Russia's annection and to conserve and promote the Belarusian Byzantine Catholic Church, Ruthenian Uniate Church, and the Litwanian (so called Old Belarusian) language. He also p
2 CONC romoted the idea of activisation of peasants for the cause of national liberation, the idea that was until then dominated by the gentry. He also referred to the good traditions of democracy, tolerance and freedom of the Polish-Lit
2 CONC huanian Commonwealth, as opposed to national oppression of cultures dominated by Russian Empire. After the outbreak of January Uprising he was involved in the secret Prowincjonalny Litewski Komitet w Wilnie. However, after initial
2 CONC  successes against the Russian armies, the Russians moved a 120, 000 men strong army to the area, and the revolutionaries started to lose most of the skirmishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, Austria granted autonomy to Galicia, making Polish the official language and establishing the provincial diet. After the Ausgleich of 1867, Galicia found itself in Cisleithania - the Austrian
2 CONC  part of Austria-Hungary. As this form of Austrian rule was more benevolent than that exercised by Russia and Prussia, Kraków became a Polish national symbol, and a center of culture and art. Trubecki was a member of the Międzynar
2 CONC odowa Socjalno-Rewolucyjna Partia Proletariat and the Polska Partia Socjalno-Rewolucyjna Proletariat. He also helped publishing "Proletariat", one of the first clandestine newspapers in Polish. Trubecki was a correspondent of anar
2 CONC chist press from 1901 on, under the different names, e.g. in Freiheit, Neues Leben, Der Anarchist, Der Freie Arbeiter; a contributor of the Polish anarcho-syndicalist monthly "Wolny Swiat" in 1904; for a while active in Bohemia wh
2 CONC ere he contributed for "Der Generalstreik". In 1905 Trubecki went to revolutionary Warsaw, Congress Poland (Russian Revolution of 1905). He was imprisoned in Warsaw. Trubecki fled arrest in April 1906 and went to Zürich, where he 
2 CONC became a contributor of "Der Weckruf" and a member of Jan Machajski's squad in Geneva; expelled from Switzerland, lived in several European countries and returned to Congress Poland; active in the Polish-Lithuanian underground res
2 CONC istance. 
2 CONT
