1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Clément Trubecki''' h. Pogoń Litewska (b. circa 1884, Łeba – date of death unknown) 
2 CONT
2 CONT A Polish-Lithuanian nobleman, Austro-Hungarian corvette captain, and diplomatic liaison. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT Noble Origins and Revolutionary Influence 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clément Trubecki was born in the historic town of Łeba on the Baltic Sea in the former Province of Pomerania in Prussia to Prince Nester Trubecki and Emilia Trubecka. He was descended from the ancient and distinguished princely ho
2 CONC use of Trubecki (or Troubetzkoy), a noble lineage rooted at the crossroads of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and Muscovy. The Trubecki family, bearing the Pogoń Litewska coat of arms, had historically straddled the fault lines of em
2 CONC pires, cultures, and ideologies.
2 CONT Clément's father, Prince Nester Trubecki, was a legendary figure in radical intellectual circles—an anarchist, freethinker, and fighter for the liberation of oppressed peoples from Poland to Livonia. Through his father, Clément wa
2 CONC s instilled with a firm ideological foundation that emphasized liberty, humanism, and resistance to tyranny. The younger Trubecki would carry this ideological compass with him throughout his life—through imperial service, undergro
2 CONC und activity, and exile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT Education and Naval Career 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clément was raised in an environment of politically engaged aristocracy, moving between Kraków and Lublin, where family estates and salons hosted spirited discussions on politics, literature, and national identity. By the end of t
2 CONC he 1890s, he had been admitted to the prestigious Theresian Military Academy (Theresianische Militärakademie) in the Burg Wiener Neustadt—one of the Habsburg Empire’s foremost officer schools. There he received a rigorous educatio
2 CONC n in military science and leadership, standing out for his intellect and multilingual fluency. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He furthered his studies in law and military strategy at the University of Lwów (then part of the Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria) and the Imperial University of Dorpat, then a renowned hub of classical education within the Russi
2 CONC an Empire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon graduation, Trubecki entered naval service as a corvette captain in the Austro-Hungarian Navy, stationed in the Adriatic port of Pola (modern-day Pula, Croatia). At the time, Pola served as a key base for the Dual Monarchy’s 
2 CONC maritime ambitions. Fluent in German, Polish, Russian, and French, Clément was often tasked with duties that blended command responsibilities with diplomatic and intelligence work. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1917, amidst growing disillusionment with imperial politics and in the wake of the Russian Revolutions, he resigned his commission. He departed for France with his older brother Paweł Trubecki, settling first in the Atlantic po
2 CONC rt town of Les Sables-d'Olonne, and later returning east to Łeba, then in Prussian-controlled Pomerania. There, through his family’s extensive network, he briefly aligned with the Polish Socialist Party, aiding political refugees 
2 CONC and gathering intelligence on the shifting power dynamics in Central and Eastern Europe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT The Dark Horse of “Operation Hermione” 
2 CONT
2 CONT By late 1918, in the aftermath of the alleged execution of the Romanov family in Yekaterinburg, rumours began to circulate across the Petrograd–Omsk–Perm axis. Reports from White Army operatives and Czech Legionnaires described a 
2 CONC covert operation, suggesting that a “Prince Troubetzkoy” had been involved in an escape network for surviving members of the Imperial family. That name, as it turned out, was Clément Trubecki, sometimes operating under the name fo
2 CONC rm Troubetzkoy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his sister Benita von Pistohlkors, who was married to a distant Romanov relative, Clément established trusted contact with several key players: former imperial physicians, White officers loyal to the Tsar, and notably a di
2 CONC sillusioned Cheka operative named Vasili Vasilevitch Iakovlev. Yakovlev had previously overseen the Romanovs’ relocation from Tobolsk and was known to harbour doubts about the Bolsheviks’ methods. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Together with General Radola Gajda (a Czech commander), Cornet Sergei Vladimirovitch Markov, and Iakovlev, Trubecki helped orchestrate Operation “Hermione”—a clandestine mission carried out entirely outside formal military or dipl
2 CONC omatic channels. Trubecki operated primarily out of Odessa, Tiraspol, and later aboard French naval vessels in the Black Sea, where he facilitated the secret evacuation of Grand Duchesses Olga and Maria, and allegedly even Empress
2 CONC  Alexandra Feodorovna, under false identities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Some later testimonies linked Trubecki to a convent in Altötting, Bavaria, home to the Sisters of the Holy Cross, where a nun named Maria Konrada Grabmair—possibly Grand Duchess Maria—resided during the 1919. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT Family Life and Cultural Legacy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clément married Anna Maria Klementyna Czartoryska, daughter of a prominent Polish landowner and an accomplished salonnière with a keen passion for classical music. Their marriage was both intellectually vibrant and socially conseq
2 CONC uential. Anna Maria hosted political salons in Odessa and Lwów, where émigrés, revolutionaries, and aristocrats discussed justice, exile, and the future of stateless nations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had three children: 
2 CONT •	Irena Trubecka, who later married into the Wołowski family; 
2 CONT •	Helena Trubecka, a writer and teacher;
2 CONT •	Stanisław Trubecki, who preserved the family’s archives and memory during the interwar period. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clément Trubecki was one of seven siblings born to Prince Nester Trubecki and Emilia Trubecka. Clément’s siblings were also deeply involved in political and military causes: 
2 CONT
2 CONT •	Among them was his elder brother Herasim Trubecki (b. 1859, Vienna), a renowned chemist whose groundbreaking work in organic synthesis and contributions to the Baku oil industry earned him recognition across Europe and the Cauca
2 CONC sus. A passionate intellectual shaped by his father’s revolutionary legacy, Herasim became an influential figure in both scientific and reformist circles, advocating for sustainability and education throughout his career. 
2 CONT •	Anita and Benita Trubecka were cultural figures in Berlin and Vienna, moving in aristocratic and artistic circles. 
2 CONT •	Ewita Bystram (née Trubecka, b. 1869, Bobolice near Myszków) married Witold Michał Bystram h. Tarnawa and raised a family rooted in noble traditions. Known for her quiet resilience and strong sense of familial duty, Ewita was th
2 CONC e matriarch of the Bystram family, mother to Franciszek and Rozalia, and maintained close ties with her siblings throughout times of exile and political upheaval. 
2 CONT •	Paweł Trubecki served in the Belarusian Special Detachment in the Baltics (Asobny Atrad Biełaruskaj Demakratyčnaj Respubliki ŭ Baltyi) during the Estonian War of Independence; 
2 CONT •	Nikodem Trubecki, known under the alias Nikolaj Ivanovitch Tulskij, captained the icebreaker Ermak during the harsh winters of 1921–1922, aiding in the evacuation of White Russian officers to Baltic ports. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________
2 CONT Fate Unknown: Vanished into Exile or Legend? 
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1922, Clément Trubecki vanished from official records. Various sources place him either in the Free City of Danzig or in Estonia, where his brother Paweł had settled. Others claim he lived in Montreux or Lausanne, Switzerlan
2 CONC d, and participated in unofficial gatherings of exiled Romanovs and monarchist sympathizers during the 1930s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Some researchers believe he assumed one or more aliases during this time—C. Lubomirski or Trumbach are two names that appear in border registries and hotel logs in Central Europe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His final fate remains a mystery—as does the full extent of his involvement in one of the most controversial episodes of twentieth-century Russian history. Was he merely a loyal courier, or did he help spirit away the last Romanov
2 CONC s under the nose of a crumbling empire? History has yet to decide. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________
